### The Numbers Don't Lie

This year alone, 807 products have been pulled from shelves, marking a significant portion of the 1165 total recalls on record. Class I recalls--the most severe kind--make up 234 of this year's cases, underscoring the ongoing risks consumers face.

However, a repeat offender stands out: D'Dioses Fruit Pops, Inc. has issued an alarming series of recalls this month. The issue? Serious labeling failures that could have life-threatening consequences for some consumers.

### What Happened?

On July 22, 2026, D'Dioses Fruit Pops, Inc. announced Class I recalls for 11 individual products. These items, marketed as ice pops, were found to contain undeclared allergens such as milk, pecans, and pistachios, alongside artificial dyes Yellow #5 and Red #40. Such undeclared ingredients pose significant risks to those with allergies or sensitivities.

The recalled products include:

- Ice Pop, D'Dioses Nuez (UPC 827912008548)
- Ice Pop, D'Dioses Tamarindo (UPC 827912008593)
- Ice Pop, D'Dioses Arroz con Leche (UPC 710594511843)
- And others, totaling 11 varieties.

What makes this particularly disconcerting is the sheer volume of affected products. With 16 recalls and counting, D'Dioses has garnered a score of 87 out of 100 on our "brands to watch" list, solidifying its reputation as a repeat offender.

### Why It Matters

Undeclared allergens are no laughing matter. For individuals with allergies, consuming these products could lead to severe reactions, hospitalization, or worse. The inclusion of artificial dyes, while not inherently dangerous, raises additional questions about transparency in food labeling.

### Lessons Learned?

D'Dioses is far from the only company with recall issues, but their case highlights the importance of rigorous quality control and accurate labeling. For consumers, this serves as a stark reminder to always double-check product labels and stay informed about recalls.

### Moving Forward

While the FDA and other agencies work to ensure accountability, companies like D'Dioses must step up their game. It's high time for stricter oversight and penalties to prevent such lapses from endangering public health.

As always, we'll keep a close eye on developments and report back with updates. Stay safe and stay informed.
